Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 13:15:06 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08060/11362] [outer-loop] security audit fix 4/5: dependency
 advisories 53 -> 5

Full OSV sweep of all 225 installed packages: 53 advisories across 10 packages
down to 5 across 3. Cleared: GitPython (9, incl. command injection and env-var
exfiltration via create_remote), pillow (26, incl. OS command injection),
pyasn1 (5), mcp, msgpack, httplib2, pydantic-settings.

pyproject floors raised so a venv rebuild cannot silently regress the two that
are declared dependencies:
  mcp    >=1.0     -> >=1.28.1,<2   (GHSA-vj7q-gjh5-988w)
  pillow >=10.0    -> >=12.3.0

The <2 cap on mcp is deliberate. The unconstrained upgrade pulled mcp 2.0.0, a
major rewrite dragging in a different httpx stack (httpx2/httpcore2/mcp-types).
The advisory is fixed in 1.28.1, so taking 2.x would have been acquiring a
migration as a side effect of a security bump. Pinned to 1.29.0.

Three remain, accepted with reasons recorded in ops/known-issues.md:

  setuptools 81.0.0 -- fix is in 83.0.0 but torch 2.11 requires setuptools<82,
      a real resolver conflict, not a hypothetical. 81.0.0 does fix the serious
      one (GHSA-5rjg-fvgr-3xxf, network-reachable path traversal -> arbitrary
      file write). What remains is local, UI-required, and only affects BUILDING
      an sdist.
  diskcache 5.6.3 -- no fix published; 5.6.3 is latest. Transitive, zero direct
      importers in this tree.
  torch 2.11.0+cu128 -- fix is in 2.13.0, but this is a CUDA-built wheel and per
      CLAUDE.md a plain reinstall silently swaps in the CPU-only build, which
      would invalidate the GPU provenance of every live_llm_inference artifact.
      Needs a deliberate verified CUDA rebuild, not a pip upgrade.

GPU stack verified unchanged before and after (this is the failure mode CLAUDE.md
warns about): llama_cpp GPU offload True, torch 2.11.0+cu128, 2 CUDA devices,
pip check clean. torch/torchvision/torchaudio/llama-cpp-python/jax were pinned in
a pip --constraint file throughout so the resolver could not replace them.

Also records that `pip-audit -r` cannot audit this venv at all (it forces a
resolution that fails on the local +cu128 torch), and that bare `uvx pip-audit`
audits its own isolated environment and misreports "No known vulnerabilities".

Adds REQ-SEC-001 to the safety spec, covering fix 3/5's deserialization
confinement, per the spec-anchored workflow.

